<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/programming-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Programming is giving unambiguous instructions to a machine that does exactly what you say —
    not what you mean. Four constructs carry almost every program ever written:
    <strong>variables</strong> (remember), <strong>conditionals</strong> (decide),
    <strong>loops</strong> (repeat), and <strong>functions</strong> (package). Learn these in any
    language — Python is the friendliest first — and every other language is a dialect with
    different punctuation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four constructs — a complete program's skeleton" widget="equationStep" config={progCfg} />

  <h2>Learning the craft properly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read errors as clues, not insults.</strong> A traceback names the file, line and complaint. Beginners fear red text; professionals read it first.</li>
    <li><strong>Trace by hand.</strong> Write three values on paper and walk your loop one step at a time. Most bugs surrender to manual tracing before any debugger opens.</li>
    <li><strong>Naming is half the program.</strong> x vs customer_total: the computer doesn't care, but the human reading it in six months is you.</li>
    <li><strong>Types matter even in loose languages:</strong> "5" + 3 is "53" in JavaScript and an error in Python. Knowing your data's type prevents a whole genre of bug.</li>
    <li><strong>Small and working beats big and broken:</strong> write two lines, run them, repeat. Programs are grown, not written.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Python Software Foundation tutorial; Kernighan & Ritchie, The C Programming Language (the classic); Zed Shaw, Learn Python the Hard Way.</p>
</TopicPage>
